{
  "dom_id": "step-q132327",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The output voltage of laptop AC adapters is normally distributed with a mean of $19$ volts and a standard deviation $0.05$ volts. What is the probability that the output voltage of a randomly selected adapter is less than $18.97$ volts?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the voltage (in volts) of a randomly selected adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{0.05}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<18.97).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<18.97)=P(X\\le 18.97)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 18.97) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{18.97-19}{0.05}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.6) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.6)=0.2743≈0.27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the output voltage of a randomly selected adapter is less than $18.97$ volts is approximately $0.27.$",
